"Extends the box plot with nested tail quantile intervals to reveal distribution structure in large samples.\n\nUse when: A large dataset requires more tail detail than a quartile box provides. Several group distributions must be compared compactly without smoothing.\n\nAvoid when: Small samples make deep tail quantiles unstable and overly precise.\n\nVisual grammar: Nested box endpoints encode progressively more extreme quantiles. Box widths distinguish quantile depths according to an explicitly chosen width rule.\n\nBuild: Choose a depth policy based on sample size and tail reliability. Compute nested quantile intervals around the median for each group. Draw outer intervals behind inner boxes and identify median and outside points.\n\nCheck: Check nested intervals remain correctly ordered and centered by quantile probability. Verify the deepest displayed quantiles have adequate sample support. State the depth and width rules used across groups.\n\nPitfalls: Readers may mistake width for density unless the rule is explained. Using equal depth for radically different sample sizes can suggest unjustified tail precision.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic request-duration tails for three services with millions of observations and labeled median intervals.\n\nAlternatives: Box plot, Violin plot, ECDF."
